<h2>1. Global Shortcuts (Work on Any Page)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Action</th><th>Windows/Linux</th><th>Mac</th></tr>
<tr><td>New Project</td><td>Ctrl + N</td><td>Cmd + N</td></tr>
<tr><td>Save Project</td><td>Ctrl + S</td><td>Cmd + S</td></tr>
<tr><td>Save As</td><td>Ctrl + Shift + S</td><td>Cmd + Shift + S</td></tr>
<tr><td>Import Media</td><td>Ctrl + I</td><td>Cmd + I</td></tr>
<tr><td>Undo / Redo</td><td>Ctrl + Z / Ctrl + Shift + Z</td><td>Cmd + Z / Cmd + Shift + Z</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keyboard Customization</td><td>Ctrl + Alt + K</td><td>Cmd + Opt + K</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch to Media Page</td><td>Shift + 2</td><td>Shift + 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch to Cut Page</td><td>Shift + 3</td><td>Shift + 3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch to Edit Page</td><td>Shift + 4</td><td>Shift + 4</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch to Fusion Page</td><td>Shift + 5</td><td>Shift + 5</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch to Color Page</td><td>Shift + 6</td><td>Shift + 6</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch to Fairlight Page</td><td>Shift + 7</td><td>Shift + 7</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch to Deliver Page</td><td>Shift + 8</td><td>Shift + 8</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2. Playback & Marking (Edit / Cut Page)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
<tr><td>Play forward / reverse (JKL shuttle)</td><td>J (reverse), K (pause), L (forward)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mark In / Out point</td><td>I / O</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blade tool (cut clip at playhead)</td><td>B</td></tr>
<tr><td>Split clip at playhead</td><td>Ctrl/Cmd + \ (backslash)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ripple delete (remove and close gap)</td><td>Shift + Delete/Backspace</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lift (remove, leave gap)</td><td>Delete / Backspace</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insert edit</td><td>F9</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overwrite edit</td><td>F10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add edit marker</td><td>M</td></tr>
<tr><td>Go to next/previous edit point</td><td>Up / Down arrow</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="note">F9 and F10 behavior (insert vs overwrite) is one of the most commonly confused pairs under deadline pressure — worth drilling separately.</p>

<h2>3. Color Page (Node & Grading Shortcuts)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
<tr><td>Add serial node</td><td>Alt/Opt + S</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add parallel node</td><td>Alt/Opt + P</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add layer node</td><td>Alt/Opt + Y</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add node (default)</td><td>Ctrl/Cmd + D</td></tr>
<tr><td>Toggle grade on/off (bypass)</td><td>D</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reset node grade</td><td>Shift + D (varies by build — verify in your Keyboard Customization panel)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grab still / Save still</td><td>Grab: Ctrl/Cmd + Grab Still shortcut in Gallery panel</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="note">The Color page has dozens of unassigned actions (panel toggles, AI tools). Open Keyboard Customization and filter by "Unassigned" to see what you can bind yourself.</p>

<h2>4. Fusion Page</h2>
<p>Most individual Fusion node types don't have a dedicated hotkey — you'll use the node search popup for almost everything:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
<tr><td>Open node search / add tool</td><td>Shift + Spacebar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fit view to nodes</td><td>Shift + F (varies) — or scroll/zoom on trackpad</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5. Fairlight Page</h2>
<p>Fairlight has some of the largest gaps in Resolve's default shortcut coverage — most panel toggles, EQ, and AI audio tools have no hotkey by default. Common ones:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
<tr><td>Solo track</td><td>S</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mute track</td><td>M</td></tr>
<tr><td>Normalize audio</td><td>Right-click clip &gt; Normalize (no default hotkey)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6. Switching From Another Editor?</h2>
<p>DaVinci Resolve ships two built-in keyboard presets: <strong>DaVinci Resolve</strong> (default) and <strong>DaVinci Resolve Cut</strong> (optimized for the Cut page and Speed Editor hardware). You can also import a Premiere Pro, Final Cut Pro 7, or Avid Media Composer keyboard layout from the Keyboard Customization panel (<kbd>Ctrl/Cmd+Alt/Opt+K</kbd>) — a huge time-saver if you're switching NLEs.</p>

<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Does Blackmagic publish an official shortcuts PDF for Resolve 21?</strong><br>
No — only older per-version PDFs on their documents server. The in-app Keyboard Customization panel is the only reference guaranteed to match your exact build.</p>

<p><strong>Can I use my old Premiere Pro shortcuts in Resolve?</strong><br>
Yes — Keyboard Customization lets you import a Premiere Pro, FCP7, or Avid preset directly.</p>

